    "passage": "While spinning on its axis, Earth wobbles like an off-kilter top. Melting ice, ocean currents, and even natural events such as hurricanes will cause the poles to wander. Like an unbalanced ball, Earth’s spin axis moves unsteadily with its center [[1]] toward Iceland at a rate of about 9 centimeters per year. Recently, researchers have discovered that a significant portion of the polar drift can be attributed to human activities, [[2]] the extraction of groundwater for agricultural irrigation and drinking water.\nTo know more about the polar wander, in a model designed to explain the observed polar movements from 1993 to 2010, scientists [[3]] factors like dam-induced reservoir changes and ice sheet melting. Nevertheless, these factors [[4]] couldn’t explain the observed polar motion. When the researchers took into consideration the estimated 2150 gigatons of pumped groundwater from the same period, the model’s predictions corresponded [[5]] to the actual polar motion, indicating that groundwater removal seemed to have caused Earth’s poles to shift during that time.\nEven though the extra wobble seems small and insignificant, the research findings can help scientists gauge other phenomena that may directly impact human beings in the not too distant future.",
